---
import PageShell from "#src/layouts/PageShell.astro";
import { buildNewsArticle, getSiteUrl } from "#src/lib/structured-data.js";
import { newsMeta } from "./_metadata";

const siteUrl = getSiteUrl(Astro.site);
const structuredData = buildNewsArticle({
  siteUrl,
  pageUrl: new URL(newsMeta.href, siteUrl).toString(),
  headline: newsMeta.title,
  description: newsMeta.description,
  datePublished: newsMeta.date,
});
---

<PageShell
  title={newsMeta.title}
  description={newsMeta.description}
  pagePath={newsMeta.href}
  canonicalUrl={newsMeta.href}
  structuredData={structuredData}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "News", href: "/news/" },
    { label: newsMeta.title, href: newsMeta.href, current: true },
  ]}
  heading={newsMeta.title}
>
  <Fragment slot="header">
    <p class="text-muted">
      <time datetime={newsMeta.date}>October 24, 2026</time>
    </p>
    <p class="lead mb-0">
      A question about policing rarely ends with one record. You might start
      with a city, find a department, look up a person, and then follow a link
      to a report or court case. The new navigation on PoliceConduct.org helps
      you follow those connections.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="py-vh-2">
    <p>
      The site brings local browsing and linked records together. You can start
      with the place you know, then move between the agencies, people, and
      records connected to it. Each page gives you a way to keep exploring
      without starting your search over.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ipc-section-title mt-5">Start with a place or a search</h2>
    <p>
      On the <a href="/">home page</a>, use the map to choose a state with
      records. From there, follow the links through counties or other local
      areas, cities and towns, and agencies. When there is only one next
      destination, a link may take you straight to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The navigation trail near the top of a page shows where you are and gives
      you links back to broader views. Location pages also offer lists and jump
      menus, so you can move directly to a place instead of opening each page in
      turn.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you already know what you are looking for, use the search box in the
      site header or open <a href="/find-records/">Find Records</a>. Federal
      agencies have their own list on the home page, with links to their related
      places and offices.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ipc-section-title mt-5">Follow people across agencies</h2>
    <p>
      An agency page connects you to the people listed in its records. A
      person's profile brings together records linked to that person, including
      agency history, licenses, discipline, education and training, and civil
      cases where those records are present.
    </p>
    <p>
      That history matters when someone has worked for more than one agency. You
      can follow a person across recorded assignments rather than treating each
      department's listing as a separate person. Dates help show when an
      assignment or event took place.
    </p>
    <p>
      State pages also link to the licensing authority. For example, the{" "}
      <a href="/mn/licensing-authority/">Minnesota licensing authority page</a>{" "}
      connects discipline records to individual profiles. A record on a person's
      profile does not, by itself, mean it belongs to every agency where that
      person has worked.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ipc-section-title mt-5">Connect reports and court cases</h2>
    <p>
      Reports and civil cases provide other paths through the record. Their
      links let you move between an account or case and the people and agencies
      connected to it. Follow source links to read the material behind a record
      and check its dates and details.
    </p>
    <p>
      A case can have more than one connection. It may appear in a location view
      because the incident took place there, and on a person's or agency's page
      because of the people linked to the case. These are different ways to find
      the same case, not separate cases to add together.
    </p>
    <p>
      A link explains how records are connected. It does not turn a reported
      claim into a finding. Read the report or case details to understand what
      the source says and what outcome is recorded.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ipc-section-title mt-5">Read the page behind the number</h2>
    <p>
      Summary numbers help you choose where to look next. Open the linked
      records to see which people, dates, agencies, or cases a number covers. A
      count for a place and a count for an agency may describe different groups
      of records.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a simple starting point, open <a href="/tx/">Texas</a>, choose a
      county and place, and open an agency. From its personnel list, choose a
      person and explore the records on that profile. Use the navigation trail
      to return to the broader area whenever you want to change direction.
    </p>

    <h2 class="ipc-section-title mt-5">Help make the connections useful</h2>
    <p>
      The goal is to make it easier to explore the public record and understand
      how its pieces fit together. That includes records of help and
      professionalism as well as concerns and harm.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can <a href="/report/new/">share your experience</a>, use the
      correction links on agency and personnel pages, or report an issue through
      the site footer. For more about how the site organizes records, visit{" "}
      <a href="/data-and-methods/">Data and Methods</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>
